migrants on board this tiny dinghy  paddled desperately away from france  a french maritime vehicle following  closely behind it escorting  possibly towards dover  earlier in the day several more tightly  packed rescue boats arrived at the port  of dover  with around 100 people on board among  them  were children there's been a significant  rise in the number of migrants and  refugees crossing the channel  this year alone 2 000 people have made  that journey  that's three times higher than last year  these are people  who have already been through terrible  things and they're people that we know  and that we talk to  seeing them having to risk their lives  like this is very very distressing  they try to deter them but the  deterrents don't work because the things  that they are running from  don't go away and they simply can't go  back being a refugee literally means  that to go back can mean death  so they can't go back they can't stay  they have no other choice apart from to  try and come  the government has recently announced  changes to the asylum process  it will mean that people who have paid a  people smuggler  or have come through what the government  deems as a safe country  will find it incredibly hard to claim  asylum in the uk  that's been criticized by rights groups  but the home office says the intention  is to make the system fairer  one thing is abundantly clear there are  still  many people who are willing or feel that  they have no choice but to make this  perilous journey  jemima walker sky news dover
 

